INT. FLOODED CHAPEL CRYPT - NIGHT

Ankle-deep black water. Stone arches. Lanterns on ropes. Names of the dead carved on every column. Something drips, then something larger drips.

HARLAN (50s, woolen coat, reading glasses) steadies a lantern. PENNY (30s, hiking boots, camera bag) wades behind him. NOLA (20s, white nightgown under a borrowed jacket, bare feet) clings to the pillar.

HARLAN
Everyone stay together.

PENNY
Harlan, we are together. There are three of us.

NOLA
Four.

HARLAN
What?

NOLA
There were four when we came down.

PENNY
Nola, honey, count again.

NOLA
One, two, three...

HARLAN
Don't.

PENNY
Let her.

NOLA
Four.

Harlan raises the lantern. The crypt stretches out, silent. Shadows lengthen between the columns.

HARLAN
(muttering)
Just the damp. Just the damp.

PENNY
Who are you talking to?

HARLAN
Myself.

NOLA
He's talking to it.

PENNY
Nola!

NOLA
It's listening.

The water ripples. Not from their legs. From the far end of the crypt, a slow wake spreads toward them.

HARLAN
Everyone back to the stairs. Now.

PENNY
Which way is that?

NOLA
Behind you.

Penny turns. The stairs are gone. Only wall, and in the wall a carved name that was not there a minute ago.

PENNY
Oh my God. That's my name.

HARLAN
That's impossible.

NOLA
It's the fourth.

HARLAN
(to himself)
Count the lanterns. Count the lanterns.

The lanterns, one by one, go out.
